t.suptitle('Epistemic uncertainty should be higher and more separated for OOD inputs')\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()\n","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-08-23T20:28:21.106063Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-08-23T20:28:21.1065Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-08-23T20:28:21.835818Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-08-23T20:28:21.106452Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-08-23T20:28:21.834842Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 1600x400 with 3 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":14},{"id":"00311ea6","cell_type":"markdown","source":"If ABNN is working as intended, its two histograms (CIFAR-10 vs. SVHN) should be **more\nseparated** than the deterministic model's -- since the deterministic model only has one\nforward pass, its \"epistemic uncertainty\" is trivially always 0 (no disagreement possible\nwith a single pass), which is exactly why a deterministic model *can't* do OOD detection this\nway at all. That comparison alone is the core argument for the whole project.\n","metadata":{}},{"id":"991b63ed","cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 7. Notes and honest caveats\n\n- **This is a compact ABNN reproduction, not a paper-scale reproduction.**\n  The implementation now includes the main ABNN ingredients used in this notebook:\n  Bayesian Normalization Layers, MAP-style regularization, class-dependent random\n  loss perturbations, multiple independently adapted ABNN modes, and M x L\n  stochastic predictive averaging.\n\n- **MAP prior:** a Gaussian parameter prior is represented through L2-style\n  weight decay on the trainable ABNN parameters.\n\n- **Random loss perturbation:** each ABNN mode receives its own fixed class-dependent\n  `eta` vector during adaptation. This makes the training objective slightly different\n  across modes, following the paper's motivation for avoiding collapse to the same\n  local minimum.\n\n- **Multi-mode inference:** ABNN predictions are averaged over\n  `N_ABNN_MODELS × N_MC_SAMPLES` stochastic predictions. With the current settings,\n  this is `3 × 10 = 30` predictions per input.\n\n- **Small-scale by design:** `N_TRAIN=2000`, `N_TEST=500`, `N_OOD=500`,\n  and short adaptation are being used to iterate quickly. These are development-scale\n  experiments, not paper-matching numbers.\n\n- **OOD evaluation:** CIFAR-10 is treated as in-distribution and SVHN as\n  out-of-distribution. The actual SVHN class labels are not used for OOD detection.\n\n- **Metrics:** Accuracy and NLL measure predictive performance; ECE measures\n  calibration; OOD AUROC and FPR95 measure whether epistemic uncertainty can\n  separate ID from OOD inputs.\n\n- **Next step:** after this multi-mode ABNN result is verified, we should focus on\n  analyzing the uncertainty behavior and making the experiment/reporting stronger\n  rather than continuing to add unrelated model components.\n","metadata":{}}]}